use Libraries.Web.Page.all
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.ComputerScienceStandards2026
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.QuorumStandards

/*
    Tutorial: Accessibility, part of the user interface series.
*/
class UserInterfacesAccessibility is QuorumPage
    // The site's sets of standards, built once for every page.
    QuorumStandards standards

    on create
        SetLocation("tutorials/ui/accessibility.html")
        SetShortTitle("Accessibility")
        SetSectionName("User Interfaces")
    end

    action Setup
        // For search engines and link previews.
        SetDescription("An overview of accessibility for user interfaces: the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG), color and contrast, the devices people use to reach a computer, and a map of Quorum's Technical Accessibility Track.")

        Title("Accessibility")

        Overview()
        WarmUp()
        Explore()
        Engage()
        WrapUp()
    end

    action Overview
        Section("Overview")
        Paragraph():Strong("Accessibility"):Text(" means that people with disabilities can use what you
build: they can find it, understand it and operate it. Throughout this
series, you have given controls names, set the focus order and checked
colors. Those are all parts of accessibility. This last tutorial steps
back to show the big picture: the international standard for accessible
technology, why color and contrast matter, and the many devices people use
to reach a computer. Quorum's website has a whole
"):Link("Technical Accessibility Track", "/lessons/accessibility.html"):Text(" that teaches
each of these topics in depth. This page is a map of it.")

        Subsection("Goals")
        Paragraph("You have the following goals for this tutorial:")
        Goals()
        ListItem("Describe what the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) are, their
four principles and their three levels")
        ListItem("Explain why color can't be the only way to show information, and what a
contrast ratio measures")
        ListItem("Describe how people use screen readers, braille displays, eye trackers,
switches and other devices")
        ListItem("Imagine using everyday products with different disabilities, and suggest
changes that would help")
        ListItem("Find the tutorials in the Technical Accessibility Track that go deeper")
    end

    action WarmUp
        Section("Warm up")
        Paragraph("Think of a time a device was hard for you to use: a phone screen in bright
sunlight, a video in a loud room, a game with one hand while the other held
something, or a form with confusing directions. What made it hard? What
would have helped? For some people, a problem like this is not a moment
but every day.")

        Subsection("Vocabulary")
        Paragraph("You will be learning about the following vocabulary words:")
        Table("Vocabulary", "media/userInterface/Accessibility/Vocabulary.csv")

        Subsection("CSTA Standards")
        Paragraph("This tutorial covers the following "):Link("CSTA standards", "https://csteachers.org/pk12standards/view/"):Text(":")
        ComputerScienceStandards2026 computerScience = standards:GetComputerScience2026()
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_IMPACTS_OF_ALGORITHMS_AND_DESIGN_9)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:MIDDLE_SCHOOL_IMPACTS_OF_ALGORITHMS_AND_DESIGN_9)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:MIDDLE_SCHOOL_IMPACTS_OF_COMPUTING_SYSTEMS_37)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_IMPACTS_OF_ALGORITHMS_AND_DESIGN_11)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:MIDDLE_SCHOOL_TESTING_AND_REFINING_20)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_SOFTWARE_DEVELOPMENT_USER_EXPERIENCE_4)
        StandardsList(computerScience)
    end

    action Explore
        Section("Explore")
        WhoAccessibilityIsFor()
        TheStandard()
        ColorAndContrast()
        HowPeopleUseComputers()
        WhatQuorumDoes()
        TheTrack()
    end

    private action WhoAccessibilityIsFor
        Subsection("Who accessibility is for")
        Paragraph("Disabilities affect how people sense, move and think. The W3C, the group
that writes many web standards, groups them as auditory (hearing),
cognitive and learning, physical, speech, and visual (seeing)
("):Link("W3C", "https://www.w3.org/WAI/people-use-web/abilities-barriers/"):Text("). Some
are permanent, some come with age, and some last only a while, like a
broken arm. The same design choices also help people in hard situations,
such as reading a screen in bright sunlight. When a program is accessible,
more people can use it.")
    end

    private action TheStandard
        Subsection("WCAG: the standard")
        Paragraph("The "):Strong("Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG)"):Text(" are the international
standard for accessible web content. They are written by the W3C's Web
Accessibility Initiative, and the current version, WCAG 2.2, was published
on October 5, 2023
("):Link("W3C", "https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/"):Text("). WCAG is written
for the web, but its ideas are used for apps and other programs too.")

        Paragraph("WCAG is organized under four "):Strong("principles"):Text(". Content must be:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("Perceivable:"):Text(" people can sense it, for example by reading text that a
screen reader can speak, or captions for a video;")
        ListItem():Strong("Operable:"):Text(" people can use every control, for example from the
keyboard, with enough time, and without flashing that could cause
seizures;")
        ListItem():Strong("Understandable:"):Text(" text and controls make sense and behave in ways
people expect, with help when they make a mistake;")
        ListItem():Strong("Robust:"):Text(" assistive technologies, such as screen readers, can read the
content reliably.")

        Paragraph("Each principle has testable rules called "):Strong("success criteria"):Text(", such as
1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum). Each one has a "):Strong("conformance level"):Text(": A, AA or
AAA. Level A is the most basic, and AAA is the strictest. AA is the level
most laws and policies ask for. The track's tutorials walk through the
success criteria one section at a time:
"):Link("WCAG Section 1A and 2A", "/tutorials/accessibility/wcag2.2A1-2.html"):Text(",
"):Link("WCAG Section 3A and 4A", "/tutorials/accessibility/wcag2.2A3-4.html"):Text(",
"):Link("WCAG Section 1AA and 2AA", "/tutorials/accessibility/wcag2.2AA1-2.html"):Text(" and
"):Link("WCAG Section 3AA and 4AA", "/tutorials/accessibility/wcag2.2AA3-4.html"):Text(".
The first of these also explains the laws in the United States and Europe
that refer to WCAG.")
    end

    private action ColorAndContrast
        Subsection("Color and contrast")
        Paragraph("Color is a powerful way to show information, but it can't be the only way.
Some people see colors differently, and some can't see them at all. WCAG's
success criterion 1.4.1 Use of Color says color must not be the only way
information is shown. For example, a form that marks mistakes only in red
should also say what is wrong in words.")

        Paragraph():Strong("Contrast"):Text(" is how different two colors look in lightness. A "):Strong("contrast
ratio"):Text(" compares the lightness of two colors and gives a number from 1,
for two colors that are the same, to 21, for black on white. WCAG 2.2's
success criterion 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum), at level AA, asks for a ratio
of at least 4.5:1 for normal text, and at least 3:1 for large text, which
is at least 18 point, or 14 point bold
("):Link("W3C", "https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/contrast-minimum.html"):Text(").
Controls and important graphics, such as the parts of a chart, need at
least 3:1 against the colors next to them (1.4.11 Non-text Contrast).")

        Paragraph("In Quorum, the "):Code("Color"):Text(" class can measure a contrast ratio for you, as the
"):Link("Color Wells", "/tutorials/ui/colorWell.html"):Text(" tutorial showed. The track's
"):Link("Luminance and Color Contrast", "/tutorials/accessibility/luminanceandcolorcontrast.html"):Text("
tutorial explains how the ratio is calculated, and
"):Link("Color In Application", "/tutorials/accessibility/colorInApplication.html"):Text("
shows how to choose and check colors in a real design.")
    end

    private action HowPeopleUseComputers
        Subsection("How people use computers")
        Paragraph("People reach a computer in many ways. These are some of the most common
tools and settings, as the W3C describes them
("):Link("W3C, Input", "https://www.w3.org/WAI/people-use-web/tools-techniques/input/"):Text(";
"):Link("W3C, Perception", "https://www.w3.org/WAI/people-use-web/tools-techniques/perception/"):Text("):")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("The keyboard only."):Text(" Some people with physical, visual or cognitive
disabilities use only the keyboard, so every control must work without a
mouse. You can practice in
"):Link("No-Mouse Practice", "/tutorials/accessibility/nomousepractice.html"):Text(".")
        ListItem():Strong("Screen readers"):Text(" turn what is on the screen into speech or braille.
Common ones include NVDA, JAWS and Narrator on Windows, VoiceOver on Macs
and iPhones, TalkBack on Android and ChromeVox on Chromebooks. Practice in
"):Link("Desktop Screen Reader Practice", "/tutorials/accessibility/desktopscreenreaderpractice.html"):Text("
and
"):Link("Mobile Screen Reader Practice", "/tutorials/accessibility/mobilescreenreaderpractice.html"):Text(".")
        ListItem():Strong("Refreshable braille displays"):Text(" show a line of braille with small pins
that rise and fall, so a person can read the screen by touch. A screen
reader sends the text to the display.")
        ListItem():Strong("Screen magnifiers and display settings"):Text(" make text larger, invert
colors, raise contrast or reduce motion, for people with low vision and
others.")
        ListItem():Strong("Eye tracking"):Text(" follows where a person looks to move the pointer, and
can click when they blink.")
        ListItem():Strong("Switches"):Text(", such as large buttons or sip-and-puff tubes that a person
controls by breathing, let someone operate a computer with one or two
simple actions.")
        ListItem():Strong("Speech recognition"):Text(" lets a person speak to dictate text or give
commands.")
        ListItem():Strong("Captions"):Text(" show the speech and other sounds in a video as text, for
people who are deaf or hard of hearing.")
    end

    private action WhatQuorumDoes
        Subsection("What Quorum does, and what you do")
        Paragraph("Quorum's controls report their names, roles and states to each platform's
accessibility system, on Windows, Mac, the web, iPhone and Android, so
screen readers and other tools can find them. Your part is what this series
has taught along the way: give each control a clear name and description,
make sure the focus order makes sense, check your colors, and test with the
keyboard and, when you can, a screen reader.")

        Paragraph("Tools can help you check. Automated checkers find some problems, but not
all of them, so testing by people still matters. The track's
"):Link("Web Accessibility Checkers", "/tutorials/accessibility/webaccessibilitycheckers.html"):Text("
and
"):Link("Desktop Accessibility Inspectors", "/tutorials/accessibility/desktopaccessibilityinspectors.html"):Text("
tutorials show how to use them.")
    end

    private action TheTrack
        Subsection("The Technical Accessibility Track")
        Paragraph("The "):Link("Technical Accessibility Track", "/lessons/accessibility.html"):Text(" goes
deeper into each idea on this page:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("WCAG, section by section:"):Text(" "):Link("1A and 2A", "/tutorials/accessibility/wcag2.2A1-2.html"):Text(",
"):Link("3A and 4A", "/tutorials/accessibility/wcag2.2A3-4.html"):Text(",
"):Link("1AA and 2AA", "/tutorials/accessibility/wcag2.2AA1-2.html"):Text(",
"):Link("3AA and 4AA", "/tutorials/accessibility/wcag2.2AA3-4.html"):Text(".")
        ListItem():Strong("Using what people use:"):Text(" "):Link("No-Mouse Practice", "/tutorials/accessibility/nomousepractice.html"):Text(",
"):Link("Desktop Screen Reader Practice", "/tutorials/accessibility/desktopscreenreaderpractice.html"):Text(",
"):Link("Mobile Screen Reader Practice", "/tutorials/accessibility/mobilescreenreaderpractice.html"):Text(".")
        ListItem():Strong("Color:"):Text(" "):Link("Luminance and Color Contrast", "/tutorials/accessibility/luminanceandcolorcontrast.html"):Text(",
"):Link("Color In Application", "/tutorials/accessibility/colorInApplication.html"):Text(".")
        ListItem():Strong("Checking your work:"):Text(" "):Link("Web Accessibility Checkers", "/tutorials/accessibility/webaccessibilitycheckers.html"):Text(",
"):Link("Desktop Accessibility Inspectors", "/tutorials/accessibility/desktopaccessibilityinspectors.html"):Text(".")
        ListItem():Strong("Accessible web pages and graphics:"):Text(" "):Link("Introduction to ARIA", "/tutorials/accessibility/introductiontoARIA.html"):Text(",
"):Link("Tagging And Structure In ARIA", "/tutorials/accessibility/taggingandstructureinARIA.html"):Text(",
"):Link("Accessible Hardware Accelerated Graphics (and WebGL)", "/tutorials/accessibility/accessibleGraphicsWebGL.html"):Text(",
"):Link("Accessible Graphics within Scalable Vector Graphics (SVG)", "/tutorials/accessibility/accessibleGraphicsSVG.html"):Text(".")
    end

    action Engage
        Section("Engage")
        Paragraph("The goal of this activity is to imagine using everyday products with
different disabilities, find the barriers, and suggest fixes. Work in a
small group. Choose three products you know well, such as a video app, a
school website, a bus ticket machine, a video game, a microwave or a
program you built in this series.")

        Paragraph("Next, imagine four made-up users, each trying to do one common task with
each product, such as watching a video or buying a ticket:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("Sam"):Text(" is Deaf and uses captions.")
        ListItem():Strong("Lee"):Text(" is blind and uses a screen reader with a refreshable braille
display.")
        ListItem():Strong("Ari"):Text(" has limited use of their hands and uses a switch or eye
tracking.")
        ListItem():Strong("Jo"):Text(" has dyslexia, and long or crowded text is hard to read.")

        Paragraph("For each product and each user, walk through the task step by step, on
paper or with physical manipulatives such as index cards for each screen.
Where would they get stuck? Write down each barrier and which WCAG
principle it breaks: perceivable, operable, understandable or robust.")

        Paragraph("Last, choose the three barriers your group thinks matter most, and suggest
a fix for each. If one of the products is your own program, make the fix.
Imagining is a start, but it is not the same as the experience of people
who live with a disability. Whenever you can, ask people who use these
tools to try your work and tell you what to change.")
    end

    action WrapUp
        Section("Wrap up")
        Paragraph("Accessibility is not a feature added at the end. It is a set of choices
made all along: names, focus, color, words and testing. Think about the
products in your Engage. Which barriers came from a choice someone made on
purpose, and which came from not thinking about people different from the
designers? Which tutorial in the Technical Accessibility Track will you try
next?")
    end
end
